 things that i wanted i wanted to send  books to some people that  had been in the book and all that kind  of got stopped so  the most that i've been able to tell in  terms of impact  um well impacts in academic settings  yes i mean i've gotten you know  mostly in terms of visits to  pretty much i mean um you know india  uk i mean just like a whole bunch of  places which surprised me  but that's those are academic settings  but in terms of how it's actually  impacted like i think more of the people  that were in the book because this is an  ethnographic book so  you know you always wonder and i know  that the people in this book  specifically will read the book um  and one of them has already read the  book and she has been texting me  about you know wanting to learn more  about her own white privilege which is  very interesting i'm just like wow but  you know and i've told her you know when  i go to puerto rico we can just get  together and talk about this because  right now we're on whatsapp and that's  like a lot of texting  but um but you know but but but it was  really interesting to hear her  her reflection on it and like what she  got out of it  um at that point she was halfway through  so  i haven't heard from her since she  finished i don't know if i said anything  like  more objectionable in the last part of  it um  but i'm actually and then another person  from from brazil who works  um you know who's very always been very  interested in  in just child experts and stuff like  that  also wanted me to give a talk um  for one of her organizations so there  have been initiatives like that at this  point  um and all of that has been sort of  colored by covet  but um but yeah but it's always like  especially for interpol you know for  ethnography it's just very interesting  to see what their reaction is not only  for the broader  um academic population but also for the  people that  feel that they're in the book so yeah  great thank you let me ask all three of 