he  potential harm that it had um  you know the day we interviewed joey  gibson the next day he was a part of a  very vicious  you know kind of brawl so we were on the  one hand trying to offer  you know a nuanced account of the  conversation we had with him while  saying  this that's not meant to minimize uh  everything he's done and just  the last thing i'll say is you know i'm  doing a project now on this right-wing  radio host sunny johnson  there are long periods of time when i  listen to her  when she's  totally criticizing lecturing  white conservatives about their racism  and  i strangely identify with her like  anti-racist commitments that she's  trying to enact  even though that anti-racism is in  service of a far  different vision so i actually i don't  seem to then say this like see all the  connections we can make  as much as just that these like ideas  and possibilities are not somehow like  just  the sole possession of people who i  connect with and identify with if those  ideas are out there in the world  they may have come from our communities  and intellectual traditions but many  many people are taking them up  and i think that has to be taken  seriously otherwise all you do is just  dismiss it and say  you know you shouldn't even be having  this conversation so um  i uh that's partly what i've learned  here  thank you great i mean just listening to  that i like i  can you know it reminds me of like all  this  conversation about cultural  appropriation and to what degree are  these debates becoming this culturally  appropriated  like possibly appropriated um phenomenon  um so i think that's even though um yes  these populations were different from  what i've used  what i've done what the kind of work  that i've done before i've always really  been interested in how psychology  operates in a sociological way i mean  like how  therapy psychology affect even  acquire a material life and i think that  that continues into this project in that  sense is not super different from  that other kind of project what was  different and was very helpful  is to understand how latino latinx  studies and latin american studies  need to be examined in junction they  need to be examined together i mean  there is so much  wealth of materials on both sides that  will benefit  our reading of of the other um  so that actually led to the next the  edited volume that steve mentioned on  like this handbook of latinx studies  that i'm  doing with my colleague and friend  um so so one of the things that i felt  that came out of that process of the  of the book that that you know that i  that hadn't come from previous work  was the clarity of those connections and  those points of  of of convergence between the fields um  so i would say that that had been  something that i i  will continue to think through and try  to implement not only my research but  also in my teaching and the classes that  i put together  because i am very interested in  making sure that in classroom  discussions  we we have we have the grasp  of a broader body of literature even  when the class is latinx studies  or latin american studies as it may be  so  yeah wait um for me that this question  about how do we it's a  i hear it partly is how do we study  communities that we're not fully members  of  um and that was that was a complicated  thing for me  that mostly came down to the methods  that i chose for this book so my first  book i had done  deep hanging out of the kind that ana is  talking about i wrote about the  community that i grew up in i went to  four years  of really difficult and painful  homeowners association meetings i was  writing about white folks that i grew up  with  mostly white women um and the way that  they thought about land  and um in that sense that was a very  personal book so for the second book  though  um i was writing about people who lived  in nearby neighborhoods but were men  were older men were mexican-american or  mexican immigrant  um and so i had to think really hard  methodologically how could i offer  something useful  um and it became clear to me pretty  quickly that i could not and did not  want to do ethnography  um and that was hard for me for a little  while to kind of wrap my head around  because i had been so committed to  ethnography in the first book  um i think it was also important to say  you know there were  logistical things here that any of us  has to think about i  moved to the east coast um there are  some  rodeo and vaquero scenes here but  they're not charros and they they  it's not the place that i was trying to  write about so logistically  um it became really difficult for me to  imagine doing deep ethnography with the  folks that i was writing about but also  there had been and there still are  uh pretty um active animal welfare  campaigns directed at charros and they  are led by people who look like me  white women middle aged who grew up with  animals and so it was actually  difficult for me to gain access um and i  i worry that if i had chosen to do a  different kind of method that  so much of the work would have been  charros trying to convince me to be  their ally and their advocate  in animal welfare debates and and the  two interactions that i did have that  were  um interviews uh deep longer interviews  both took that direction really quickly  and so i had to step back from that and  figure out a different method that could  still  allow me to answer the questions that i  was curious about  but also that could be useful to the  scholarship  and so i really thought about a lot of  this book was writing in service to and  to try to be useful to  my own mentors who were mostly in  chicago studies um  and to try to do something that could be  useful to them and to the field  and it also became about writing  something useful for the  for the communities that i was writing  about but that was not ethnographic and  again that was where i was able to  identify that  there was no history of these  communities in the united states there  were there were a few ethnographies  there was no history  um and there was no comparative  geography and so i i  i landed on that and i i feel okay about  that  um even though it was such a different  thing from my first project it became  necessary because of my own subjectivity  my own location geographically subject  location in relationship to the folks i  was writing about  and i think the larger lesson that that  taught me which has become important as  i've matured a bit as a scholar is  to really um acknowledge and embrace the  fact that we can  use very different methods for different  projects and that we can feel  differently about our projects um the  first  book was very personal the second book  was less so it was more professional  um or i pursued it for more sort of  professional and political reasons  um and that that in our long life span  hopefully we would get to work on more  than one project but that  that there's room for all of that and  that we can do different kinds of work  at different moments in our life  depending on  how we're positioned and who we're in  relationship with  thank you laura and thank you anna and  thank you dan i think that's a great  place to close i  so appreciate the opportunity to hear  from all three of you  about how your books were made and again  for our audience members to our audience  members thank you for coming you can see  the  the titles of these books and some more  information about them  um in the chat if you're interested uh  laura berkoff's book is  chadros how mexican cowboys are  remapping race and american identity  dan hosang's book is producers parasites  patriots race and the new right-wing  politics of procarity  uh and uh ana ramosa's book is  parenting empires class whiteness and  the moral economy of privilege in latin  america  this has been a great conversation i  really appreciated hearing about the  contents 